#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175724 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "8c4777a6-d831-54cc-8677-b05ea45b2b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7324" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} =  4P - 0.000\\,01  P^2,  \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P = \\dfrac12a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(400\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 400\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175798 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/id #uuid "6f96059e-ce3d-5771-b3d8-15044cfd5495" :question/math-academy-id "e-9375" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of some foxes in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\ny' =0.5y - 0.002y^2, \\qquad y(0) = 25,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible fox population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.5y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(250-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 250.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $250,$ and we conclude that the number of foxes cannot exceed $250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175799 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "2096869c-2a41-51a5-b2e8-c1039af4d640" :question/math-academy-id "e-9376" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny' = 2y\\left(60 - \\frac{y}{3} \\right), \\qquad y(0) = 10\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)}?$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(60-\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\  & =2y(\\frac{180-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{3}(180-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 180.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $180.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = 180.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b47c5ce0-9a74-576f-9efe-be9d4e39d0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324624} {:answer/id #uuid "93dd6090-2cf2-536f-be00-96efad2df185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324625} {:answer/id #uuid "371fb11b-71b8-5a8e-97c7-0947148e88e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324626} {:answer/id #uuid "a08c37d3-d2b1-5b55-a954-6a6de82990a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324627} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb553d4-f9da-541f-a03d-0f5234ec801d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b47c5ce0-9a74-576f-9efe-be9d4e39d0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324624} :answer-field/id #uuid "66650271-56a9-502c-ab02-30c18494437c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a67cb8d5-18ca-5d11-9dfc-d7a49d4d64b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118560" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of mockingbirds in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.6y-0.0003{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=250,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of mockingbirds?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.6y-0.0003{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0003y(2\\,000-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and we conclude that the number of mockingbirds cannot exceed $2\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef0cc530-300e-545a-a4e3-14be21f35cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324631} {:answer/id #uuid "e46c55d3-1ccc-5291-99d2-151a4328b914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "465" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324632} {:answer/id #uuid "2927dd01-798a-53f5-a985-8571f8326f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324633} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fc8f5b-f532-55fb-9ae1-3c7aa41ab472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324634} {:answer/id #uuid "7841e048-06f5-53ad-b250-a5548e9ccc58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0fc8f5b-f532-55fb-9ae1-3c7aa41ab472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324634} :answer-field/id #uuid "6122d29a-ea43-57b8-abea-622b420c9fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe1958e-67bc-532f-b098-5d3f40081ea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-118561" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of sloths in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}),\\,y(0)=135,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible sloth population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}) \\\\  & =0.3y(\\frac{600-y}{300}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.3y}{300}(600-y) \\\\  & =0.001y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of sloths cannot exceed $600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ad7eb6-ffb1-52c9-a180-28a615a24779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324638} {:answer/id #uuid "e4aad8df-7c42-5760-a2c5-a342c292f9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324639} {:answer/id #uuid "37887efd-627a-564d-9301-0133648871a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324640} {:answer/id #uuid "63bdf1a0-4598-517a-a4f3-76ee311d5695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324641} {:answer/id #uuid "dc67f1dd-3f0b-5228-a25f-84f264f0f694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4aad8df-7c42-5760-a2c5-a342c292f9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324639} :answer-field/id #uuid "48554de2-6ac8-5e66-a659-39c3db70325e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9780978d-2902-5e41-a878-1ae5a767b35e" :question/math-academy-id "q-118577" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,200y-3{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=50\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y(400-y),\\,y(0)=50.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=400.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $400.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21616dae-175d-522a-ae8f-d9105f739576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324645} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d2a12c-ff7f-5492-a87e-500b1c5c5f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324646} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8198b8-8fed-5396-b221-276f40685a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324647} {:answer/id #uuid "71b31ba4-a582-539c-ab8e-04a486ea9ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324648} {:answer/id #uuid "a82fe3d0-64f6-5501-8852-8e0bed2f0832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a82fe3d0-64f6-5501-8852-8e0bed2f0832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324649} :answer-field/id #uuid "28626056-d143-50af-9ba9-00c25ff91414" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e28d8caa-1db8-5a53-94f9-ef625a0d5d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118580" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,750y-7{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=20\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=7y(250-y),\\,y(0)=20.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=250.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bdb9942-a7de-5864-8d72-d90587cc5813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324652} {:answer/id #uuid "380c86aa-b73f-55b6-bda0-fc83552fd6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324653} {:answer/id #uuid "f219ebeb-06d1-5ef5-abf5-21471b49bd12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324654} {:answer/id #uuid "86464e3a-4d64-50c9-a7b0-26cc7cd0a596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324655} {:answer/id #uuid "e508315a-afcd-509a-a203-026e654f205f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e508315a-afcd-509a-a203-026e654f205f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324656} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b98eb17-fa8a-55d8-96bf-a0968d39c671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45ae9233-be7e-5fe7-93a9-ab14a1115e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118581" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=720y-8{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=5\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y(90-y),\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=90.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "728f936a-83d4-555c-b174-333cdacf1433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324659} {:answer/id #uuid "6916be61-6413-539e-85e1-89d10e75ae0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324660} {:answer/id #uuid "e17ae365-2d93-5c3a-bc03-e60a377c5fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324661} {:answer/id #uuid "9314ad76-15bd-56d5-a147-f5951e911bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324662} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfa4885-bb09-5669-a4ee-f739079bb6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e17ae365-2d93-5c3a-bc03-e60a377c5fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324661} :answer-field/id #uuid "5acd10b8-b9bc-5825-b39f-d71304c79ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53898b75-e3e1-5fea-88f7-2b5d1d917e59" :question/math-academy-id "q-119842" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,350y-9{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=25\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(150-y),\\,y(0)=25.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=150.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $150.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801df426-3e35-5c86-b8da-7a5d85a0b3c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324666} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6513c8-a6f1-53df-81ac-d55079bb4e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324667} {:answer/id #uuid "6297c3b2-7f6b-53ea-8e1a-b8eb96ca74b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324668} {:answer/id #uuid "3f21711b-302e-562a-b710-4bcd13d7fd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324669} {:answer/id #uuid "b763f903-a7c1-5028-999b-000e7761709c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f21711b-302e-562a-b710-4bcd13d7fd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324669} :answer-field/id #uuid "58556f93-669f-513a-826a-f8a45ba03e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18201405-cccb-5540-8a05-32c9fb70b5f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-119846" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of dolphins in a certain ocean follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}),\\,y(0)=43,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible dolphin population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}) \\\\  & =4y(\\frac{1\\,400-y}{200}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{200}y(1\\,400-y) \\\\  & =0.02y(1\\,400-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,400,$ and we conclude that the number of dolphins cannot exceed $1\\,400.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba24c1b-3b4f-5a64-ab6a-d848e14d6558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324673} {:answer/id #uuid "666d246f-9b65-53bb-b49b-91b6150237e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324674} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc6becc-58aa-5688-9495-64431dbc583f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324675} {:answer/id #uuid "3e746939-8de3-5229-9ce2-722df8dc8c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324676} {:answer/id #uuid "23463754-4a4e-500c-8d99-48fc6c65db94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "666d246f-9b65-53bb-b49b-91b6150237e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324674} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e1015a5-8c4c-5a55-8e9b-9a782dc93cdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4a41fa6-e7d1-54e2-a912-d65af3313e72" :question/math-academy-id "q-119849" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of giraffes on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=17.2y-0.004{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=180,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of giraffes?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =17.2y-0.004{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.004y(4\\,300-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,300.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,300,$ and we conclude that the number of giraffes cannot exceed $4\\,300.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8797f27b-8233-5dbf-912c-84d80a18578a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324680} {:answer/id #uuid "60df3e3e-7a2c-5e73-b841-efa6e14c0893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324681} {:answer/id #uuid "d76a52a1-f6f0-54f0-b455-e69b65909aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324682} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcba33f-28a8-5425-bfb6-befb33a20137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324683} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfec2cd-b341-5ce0-9acf-0333667a2fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbcba33f-28a8-5425-bfb6-befb33a20137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324683} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a33dc90-988c-53e7-a11e-45433f2ccbe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3451059c-1c4e-54f9-9dc9-14e12f6043d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-119855" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of turtles $P(t)$ that developed fungus on their shells in a certain aquarium follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P-0.012{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=2.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. For which value of $P$ is the number of turtles that developed fungus growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.6P-0.012{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.012P(50-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=50.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $50,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 50=25.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8394bd-475c-5c84-b9d7-4bbc5985335d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd8394bd-475c-5c84-b9d7-4bbc5985335d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324687} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dd3e753-ef6b-56c9-a555-79e2d3aeec27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b73643b4-813a-56d4-b435-03780ad9410a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348210" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of beavers in a certain river follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0008P(1\\,400-P),\\,P(0)=60.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the beaver population growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=1400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1400,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1400=700.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b94756-c261-5ea3-b75a-f9c63a5eb728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324690} {:answer/id #uuid "570ee3d1-5bdb-5965-8659-afe2e2edb77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "570ee3d1-5bdb-5965-8659-afe2e2edb77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324691} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be9cc13-ef46-53b7-acab-fdab90e8728a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b81a0b77-b6af-5353-a3e2-62c0a28081d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-353435" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3600y-9{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40\n$$\n\nThe carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/d8f19dcb707da9aac0ff59b8127778b8b45ef4f55f3fa404b3e589e323c03905.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(400-y),\\,y(0)=40.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=400.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $400.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703cc21e-264b-5fe7-9742-04ed82869b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703cc21e-264b-5fe7-9742-04ed82869b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324694} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4938831-5227-5c39-9a6e-258e3e610976" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05786f4e-51a8-5af3-98a4-43cb1a4556c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-353477" :question/problem "The number $y(t)$ of people who have participated in a viral trend on social media follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1250y-5{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in hours, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of participants?\n\nThe maximum possible number of participants is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of participants in the trend. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1250y-5{y}^{2} \\\\  & =5y(250-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=250.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $250,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of participants is $250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9c46616-1d43-5fe5-8d11-9c61cb7c40e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9c46616-1d43-5fe5-8d11-9c61cb7c40e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324697} :answer-field/id #uuid "78bba66e-2745-50e7-996c-e592a67d174a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38a73021-b915-534e-9dcf-7cdc220a2098" :question/math-academy-id "q-353487" :question/problem "The number $P(t)$ of potential buyers in a market for a new product follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=7200P-3{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the number of potential buyers growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =7200P-3{P}^{2} \\\\  & =3P(2400-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2400,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2400=1200.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e61da589-105c-59a6-9661-47862746b2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324700} {:answer/id #uuid "74d5a8e1-d619-5d13-b65c-f798c83b281f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324701} {:answer/id #uuid "975874bc-9c40-5357-bdba-aa5a5e586006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324702} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d22db2-72f9-56eb-9446-d2100f6dc045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324703} {:answer/id #uuid "c92f64a5-3f74-513f-9bf4-ba82c1aabd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c92f64a5-3f74-513f-9bf4-ba82c1aabd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324704} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab70a64-3f86-508c-87a1-91dc20713acb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "230cd702-7176-5194-8d85-6e3150ad5869" :question/math-academy-id "q-51379" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$of rabbits in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P-0.002{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the rabbit population growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(2\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\,000=1\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e51317-34e6-56f7-85a0-e0f09f18514d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324707} {:answer/id #uuid "c846fd60-9bc4-5d94-ae81-1daa1676e579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324708} {:answer/id #uuid "56e8c70c-2d6e-5ce1-8338-20595e8fa9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324709} {:answer/id #uuid "8073da54-1255-593e-8d23-d7b8c859823a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324710} {:answer/id #uuid "dc980a47-3b1c-5500-9042-eec79ce09a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c846fd60-9bc4-5d94-ae81-1daa1676e579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324708} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc722d6-491f-559f-bbfd-dcb7c8b4e873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7032cb07-ee0e-5cbc-b7e2-ff14cc27887a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83974" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of catfishes in a particular lake follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.1P-0.002{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=5,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the catfish population growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.1P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(50-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=50.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $50,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 50=25.\n$$"}]}
